{"obligations":{"reduction":{"status":"written_and_valid","quote":null,"witness":null,"load_bearing":null},"lower_bound":{"status":"not_written","quote":"**Lemma.** For every sequence term \(x\), the set \(\sigma(x)\) is good.

**Proof.** Induction over the sequence terms in increasing order (standard; details omitted). \(\square\)","witness":"The missing all-cases statement is: for every sequence term x and every sequence term a_i, some prime p≤A divides both x and a_i. Merely naming an induction and omitting its details does not establish the induction step, especially the claimed erasure of prime factors greater than A.","load_bearing":"This finite-prime erasure is the only justification for replacing P(m) by σ(m). Without it, membership need not be determined by residues modulo the product of primes at most A."},"upper_bound":{"status":"not_written","quote":"Hence \(\sigma(m+L)=\sigma(m)\), and (3) gives
\[
m\text{ is a sequence term}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
m+L\text{ is a sequence term}. \tag{4}
\]","witness":"The required all-cases statement is that membership is invariant under m↦m+L for every m≥A. The quoted inference proves this only through (3), whose forward direction depends entirely on the unproved erasure lemma; thus no unconditional proof of (4) is written.","load_bearing":"Global membership invariance supplies the same number T of terms in every length-L interval. The final indexed identity a_{n+T}=a_n+L depends on that uniform count."}},"S_valid":0,"wrong_answer_stated":false,"notes":"Mapping: lower_bound is finite-prime erasure while retaining compatibility; upper_bound is global residue-periodicity followed by conversion to a fixed index shift. Reduction is the fixed-set scan equivalence. The counting argument is written, but remains conditional on the omitted erasure lemma."}
